// hw-pm — generated from src/hw-pm-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Hardware Product Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-pm"] = (QB["hw-pm"] || []).concat([
["Role overview", "How is hardware product management different from software product management?", "**Why they ask:** the key opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In hardware, mistakes are expensive and slow to fix — you can't push an update to a bad plastic part or a wrong chip. So decisions have to be made earlier and with more certainty. Cost of goods matters on every unit, lead times run months, and suppliers, certification and manufacturing are part of the job. I spend more time on requirements, the BOM cost and the build schedule, and I plan around fixed gates like EVT, DVT and PVT.\"\n\nTip: Irreversible decisions and unit cost are the core differences.", "B", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What carries over from software PM?","Customer discovery, prioritization and clear communication — the discipline around decisions is what changes."]}],
["Process", "Explain EVT, DVT and PVT.", "**Why they ask:** hardware development stages.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"EVT, engineering validation test, proves the design works — functional prototypes, often not in final materials. DVT, design validation test, proves the final design meets every requirement — in final materials, through reliability, environmental and certification testing. PVT, production validation test, proves the factory can build it at volume with good yield using the final process and tooling. Each stage has exit criteria, and I don't let the team move on without meeting them.\"\n\nTip: Stress exit criteria.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What happens if DVT fails a key test?","Fix the design, and decide with data whether a partial re-test is enough or a full DVT re-run is needed — usually it pushes the schedule."]}],
["Requirements", "How do you write requirements for a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** requirements drive everything.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start from customer needs and turn them into measurable requirements — battery life of 12 hours under a defined use case, drop from 1.2 meters, operating from 0 to 40 degrees. I include cost, size and weight targets, regulatory markets, and the price point. Each requirement gets a priority and a way to test it. Then I review them with engineering early, because some are expensive, and we trade off together.\"\n\nTip: 'Measurable and testable' is the key.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you handle a requirement engineering says is too costly?","Get the cost impact, check how much customers value it, and decide whether to change the target, find another approach, or accept the cost."]}],
["Cost", "How do you manage BOM cost?", "**Why they ask:** margins depend on it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I set a target cost early from the price point and margin goals, then track the BOM against it at every build. The biggest items get the most attention — often the processor, display, battery and enclosure. I work with engineering on cost-down ideas and with procurement on volume pricing and second sources. I also count the full landed cost: manufacturing, test, packaging, freight and duties, not just parts.\"\n\nTip: Mention landed cost — it shows business depth.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's a good cost-down approach late in a program?","Look for parts consolidation, cheaper second sources and packaging changes that don't need a full requalification."]}],
["Suppliers", "How do you manage contract manufacturers and suppliers?", "**Why they ask:** hardware depends on partners.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I choose partners based on capability, quality, capacity and cost, and visit the factory. We agree on clear quality metrics, test coverage and yield targets. I have regular reviews of yield, schedule and issues, and a clear escalation path. For long-lead parts I make sure orders are placed early. A good relationship — fair, direct and data-based — matters as much as the contract.\"\n\nTip: Show you are hands-on with the factory.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What is an NPI?","New product introduction — the process of moving a product from design into reliable mass production."]}],
["Supply chain", "A key chip has a 52-week lead time. What do you do?", "**Why they ask:** a real and common problem.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First, check the facts with the supplier and distributors — sometimes stock exists. Then options: secure allocation by placing a long-term order, find a second-source or alternative part and qualify it, buy from brokers carefully with authenticity testing for a bridge quantity, or redesign to use an available part. I lay out the cost, risk and schedule of each for the leadership team and decide quickly, because waiting makes it worse.\"\n\nTip: Lay out options with trade-offs and decide fast.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the risk of broker parts?","Counterfeit or poorly stored parts, so they need testing and inspection before use."]}],
["Certification", "What certifications do hardware products usually need?", "**Why they ask:** market access.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It depends on the product and markets. Most electronics need EMC approval — FCC in the US and CE in Europe — and safety certification like UL or IEC 62368. Wireless products need radio certification in every country. Batteries need shipping certification like UN 38.3. Some industries add more, like medical or automotive. I plan certification into the schedule early, because a failed test can delay launch.\"\n\nTip: Plan certification early in the schedule.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why use pre-certified radio modules?","They cut certification cost and time, which helps a lot for lower-volume products."]}],
["Prioritization", "How do you decide what features go into version one?", "**Why they ask:** hardware scope is hard to change later.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I focus on the core job the customer is buying the product for and the few things that set us apart. Features that can be added later in software can wait; anything needing hardware — a sensor, a port — must be decided now, or the hardware must have room for it. I weigh value against cost, schedule and risk, and I'm comfortable saying no to protect the launch date.\"\n\nTip: Separate hardware-dependent from software-addable features.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Would you add hardware for a future feature?","Only if it's cheap and the feature is likely — like a spare connector or extra memory — otherwise it's wasted cost on every unit."]}],
["Launch", "How do you plan a hardware product launch?", "**Why they ask:** coordination across many teams.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I work backward from the launch date: sales availability needs inventory in warehouses, which needs production weeks earlier, which needs PVT done and certifications complete. I plan the ramp with the manufacturer, build initial inventory, and coordinate packaging, documentation, support training, pricing and marketing. I also plan for problems — field returns and a quick response process in the first weeks.\"\n\nTip: Working backward from the date shows planning skill.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What is a production ramp?","Gradually increasing production volume while watching yield and quality, before running at full rate."]}],
["Metrics", "What metrics do you track for a hardware product after launch?", "**Why they ask:** product success beyond launch.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Sales and margin against plan, return and failure rates by cause, factory yield and cost per unit, customer satisfaction and reviews, and support ticket trends. A rising return rate is an early warning I act on fast — I get failed units analyzed to find the root cause.\"\n\nTip: Return rate is a key hardware metric.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's an acceptable return rate?","It varies by product category, so I compare against our own history and industry benchmarks, and act on any upward trend."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a tough trade-off you made on a hardware product.", "**Why they ask:** judgement under pressure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Late in DVT we found the battery wouldn't meet the 12-hour target in cold weather. A bigger battery meant a new enclosure and a three-month delay. I looked at usage data — most customers used it indoors — so we kept the battery, improved power management in firmware, and stated cold-weather battery life clearly. We launched on time, and returns for battery life stayed low.\"\n\nTip: Show data-driven trade-offs and honest communication.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How did you get the team to agree?","I shared the customer usage data and the cost of delay, so the decision was clear for everyone."]}],
["Closing", "Why do you want this hardware product manager role?", "**Why they ask:** motivation and fit.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I love building physical products people use every day, and hardware PM sits right where customer needs, engineering, cost and manufacturing meet. Your product line is growing into new markets, and my experience taking products through EVT to launch with contract manufacturers fits that. I'd bring disciplined stage gates and close partnership with engineering.\"\n\nTip: Link your launch experience to their stage of growth.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Do you have an engineering background?","Yes — I started as an engineer, which helps me understand trade-offs and earn the team's trust."]}]
]);

