QB["hw-pm"] = (QB["hw-pm"] || []).concat([
["Customer discovery", "How do you do customer discovery for a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** hardware mistakes are expensive, so the front end matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I talk to target users about their problem before committing to a solution, because unlike software I can't quickly pivot a product with tooling and certification sunk into it. I watch how they actually use today's solutions, quantify the pain and what they'd pay, and look for patterns across many conversations rather than one loud request. For hardware I also validate form factor, environment and constraints early with mockups, foam models or off-the-shelf prototypes. The goal is to be confident the problem and the rough solution are right before I spend the long, costly development and tooling cycle, where changing direction is painful.\"\n\nTip: Validate the problem and rough form early with cheap prototypes — hardware can't pivot cheaply after tooling.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is early validation more critical for hardware than software?","Because tooling, certification and inventory are large sunk costs with long lead times, so a wrong direction is far more expensive to correct."]}],
["PRD", "What goes into a hardware product requirements document?", "**Why they ask:** the PRD is the PM's core artifact.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It states the problem, the target user and market, and the goals, then the requirements: functional — what it must do — and the non-functional ones that dominate hardware, like size, weight, power, battery life, environmental range, reliability targets, cost, and the regulatory markets it must sell into. I separate must-haves from nice-to-haves, make each requirement testable, and tie them to the business case. For hardware I'm explicit about the target cost and the certifications, because those shape the whole design. I keep it a living document through the program and trace each requirement to a verification test, so at the end we can prove the product meets it.\"\n\nTip: Capture SWaP-C and environmental/regulatory targets as testable requirements, traced to verification — not just features.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why make each requirement testable and traced?","So at EVT/DVT you can verify the product actually meets each one, and nothing important is assumed rather than proven."]}],
["Phase gates", "How do phase-gate reviews work in a hardware NPI program?", "**Why they ask:** structured NPI is central to hardware PM.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"New product introduction runs through gates — concept, design, EVT, DVT, PVT, then mass production — and each gate is a cross-functional review with defined exit criteria before spending the next phase's money. At each gate engineering, operations, quality and the PM review whether the design is ready, risks are closed, the cost and schedule still hold, and the tests passed. The PM usually owns the gate: assembling the evidence, surfacing the real risks honestly, and driving the go, no-go, or conditional-go decision. The point is to catch problems before committing to expensive tooling or a big build, so a gate that rubber-stamps is worse than no gate.\"\n\nTip: Each gate has exit criteria and a cross-functional go/no-go — the PM owns an honest decision before the next big spend.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's a conditional-go at a gate?","Proceeding while a small, tracked set of open items gets closed in parallel, used when the risk of waiting outweighs the risk of the open items — but it must be deliberate, not a way to dodge the criteria."]}],
["Tooling", "How does tooling affect a hardware product's cost and schedule?", "**Why they ask:** tooling is a defining hardware constraint.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Hard tooling like an injection mold is a large up-front cost and a long lead time — often many weeks to months to cut and tune — and once it's cut, mechanical changes mean expensive rework or a new tool. So tooling is a major gate decision: I don't commit to production tooling until the design is validated, often using soft or prototype tooling or 3D-printed parts for early builds to keep flexibility. Tooling amortizes over volume, so it drives the volume at which the product makes financial sense. The PM has to time the tooling commitment to balance schedule pressure against the risk of changing the part after the tool is cut.\"\n\nTip: Production tooling is a big, long-lead sunk cost that resists change — commit only after the design is validated, using soft tooling early.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why use soft or prototype tooling before hard tooling?","It's cheaper and faster for early builds and lets you change the design, so you only commit to costly hard tooling once the geometry is proven."]}],
["Cost reduction", "How do you drive cost reduction over a product's life?", "**Why they ask:** sustaining margin is an ongoing PM job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"After launch I run a continuous cost-down program because margins improve by chipping at the BOM and the build. I look for the biggest-cost components first, negotiate on volume as it ramps, qualify a second source to create price competition and reduce risk, re-examine over-specified parts, consolidate components, and simplify assembly or test time. I weigh each change against the cost of re-qualifying and re-certifying it, since a cheaper part that forces a new certification may not pay off. I prioritize by savings times volume, and I make sure quality never slips for a few cents — a field failure dwarfs the saving.\"\n\nTip: Attack the biggest BOM lines, dual-source for leverage, and weigh each saving against re-qual/re-cert cost and quality risk.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why can a cheaper component still be the wrong choice?","If it triggers re-certification, re-qualification or any quality risk, those costs or a field failure can exceed the per-unit saving, especially at low volume."]}],
["Margin", "How do you think about gross margin and COGS for hardware?", "**Why they ask:** hardware PMs own the P&L realities.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Gross margin is price minus cost of goods sold, over price, and for hardware COGS is real and large — the BOM, assembly, test, yield loss, tooling amortization, freight, warranty reserve, and packaging all land in it. Unlike software there's a hard per-unit cost, so I design to a target cost from the start and protect margin across the life with cost-downs. I also account for channel margin and the fact that accessories and services can carry higher margin than the core device. I model margin at expected volumes and watch how yield, component price moves, and warranty returns erode it after launch.\"\n\nTip: COGS is real per-unit for hardware — BOM, assembly, test, yield, warranty, freight; design to a target cost and protect margin over the life.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What costs do people forget to put in hardware COGS?","Yield loss, test time, warranty reserve, freight, tooling amortization and packaging — not just the headline BOM, so true cost is higher than the parts list."]}],
["Obsolescence", "How do you manage component obsolescence and end-of-life?", "**Why they ask:** long-lived hardware outlives its parts.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Components go end-of-life while a product is still selling, so I watch for EOL and last-time-buy notices from suppliers and the lifecycle status of key parts at design time, favoring parts with long projected availability and avoiding single-sourced, soon-to-be-obsolete components. When an EOL notice comes, I weigh a last-time-buy to bridge demand against redesigning to a replacement, which may need re-qualification and re-certification. For long-life products I plan for this from the start with second sources and a monitoring process. Getting surprised by an obsolete part can halt production, so proactive lifecycle management is part of the job.\"\n\nTip: Track part lifecycle from design on; on an EOL notice, weigh a last-time-buy against a re-qualified redesign.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a last-time-buy?","A final bulk purchase of a part going end-of-life, sized to cover demand until you can redesign around it or retire the product."]}],
["Regulatory", "What regulatory approvals might a connected electronic product need, and how do you plan for them?", "**Why they ask:** certifications gate launch and shape design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It depends on the product and markets, but common ones are emissions and radio compliance — FCC in the US, CE marking covering EMC and radio for Europe — safety marks like UL or equivalent, environmental rules like RoHS and REACH restricting substances, and for radios the specific regional certifications, plus special regimes like medical or automotive if applicable. I identify the target markets and required approvals early because they shape the design and the schedule — pre-compliance testing during development, then accredited testing before launch — and they cost time and money. Missing a certification means you can't legally sell, so I build them into the plan from the start, not the end.\"\n\nTip: Identify target-market approvals (FCC/CE/UL/RoHS/radio) at design time — they shape the design and gate the launch.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why plan certification early rather than at the end?","Certifications take weeks and can force design changes; discovering a failure late means costly rework and a slipped launch, so you pre-comply during development."]}],
["Dual sourcing", "When and how do you qualify a second source for a component?", "**Why they ask:** supply resilience is a core ops-PM concern.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I dual-source parts that are critical, high-risk, single-sourced, or on long lead times, because a single supplier is a single point of failure for the whole product. Qualifying a second source means verifying the alternative truly meets the spec — not just the datasheet but real testing in the product — and that it doesn't change behavior, then getting it through any required re-certification. It costs engineering effort, so I prioritize the parts where a supply disruption would halt the line or where price leverage matters most. The payoff is resilience against shortages and price, which the last few years made very concrete.\"\n\nTip: Dual-source critical, single-sourced, long-lead parts; qualifying means real in-product testing, not just matching datasheets.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why isn't matching the datasheet enough to qualify an alternate part?","Datasheet-equivalent parts can behave differently in-circuit — timing, tolerances, quirks — so you validate the real product with the alternate before approving it."]}],
["Build plan", "How do you plan build quantities from prototype through mass production?", "**Why they ask:** the build plan is a key PM deliverable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each phase has a purpose and a quantity. A handful of prototypes prove the concept and bring up firmware. EVT, engineering validation, is a small build — tens of units — to test the design against requirements and find issues. DVT, design validation, is larger — dozens to a few hundred — to validate the design is production-ready across units, including environmental and reliability and early certification. PVT, production validation, proves the manufacturing process and tooling at near-production volume on the real line. Then mass production ramps. I size each build to get statistically meaningful results and feed the certification and reliability needs, balanced against cost and the long-lead parts I must order ahead.\"\n\nTip: Proto (concept) → EVT (tens, design) → DVT (hundreds, design+reliability) → PVT (process/tooling) → MP — each sized for its validation purpose.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the difference between DVT and PVT?","DVT validates the design is right across many units; PVT validates the manufacturing process and tooling can build it repeatably at volume on the real line."]}],
["Forecasting", "How do you forecast demand and set initial inventory for a hardware launch?", "**Why they ask:** over- or under-building both hurt badly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It's a hard balance because building too much ties up cash in inventory that may not sell and can go obsolete, while building too little means stockouts and lost sales and an unhappy channel. I build a forecast from market size, channel and sales input, comparable product launches, and any pre-order or waitlist signal, then plan an initial build with a conservative bias and the ability to ramp as real demand data arrives. I account for the long component lead times — I may have to commit to parts months before I know real demand — and I stage the ramp with the factory. I revisit the forecast constantly against actual sell-through.\"\n\nTip: Balance stockout risk against cash tied in obsolescing inventory; start conservative, commit long-lead parts early, and ramp on real sell-through.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is forecasting harder for hardware than software?","Long component lead times force you to commit to build quantities months ahead of real demand, and unsold units are costly inventory, not a free copy."]}]
]);

// Hardware Product Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-pm"] = (QB["hw-pm"] || []).concat([
["DFM", "How do you work with engineering on design for manufacturability?", "**Why they ask:** DFM is where PM, design and ops meet.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"DFM means designing the product so it can be built reliably, quickly and cheaply at volume, and my role is to make it a first-class requirement, not an afterthought. I bring manufacturing and the contract manufacturer into design reviews early so they flag parts that are hard to place, assembly steps that are error-prone, or test access that's missing, while changes are still cheap. I make sure cost, yield and test time are tracked targets alongside features, and I help the team weigh a design choice that's elegant but hard to build against one that's slightly less ideal but manufacturable. Catching DFM issues before tooling saves enormous cost.\"\n\nTip: Bring manufacturing and the CM into design reviews early, and track yield, test time and assembly cost as real targets.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a common DFM issue caught in review?","Parts too close for the pick-and-place or test probe, no test points, or an assembly step that's easy to do wrong — all cheap to fix in design, costly after tooling."]}],
["Change management", "How do you manage engineering changes once a product is in production?", "**Why they ask:** disciplined change control protects quality and cost.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Once a product ships, every change goes through formal change control — an engineering change order that documents what changes, why, the impact on cost, inventory, certification and quality, and who approves it. I care about the effectivity: when the change takes effect, what happens to existing inventory and work in progress, and whether it needs re-certification or re-qualification. Some changes are mandatory — a safety or supply issue — and some are cost-downs I can time. I weigh the benefit against the disruption and the cost of obsoleting on-hand parts. The goal is that no change sneaks in untracked, because an undocumented change is how field problems appear.\"\n\nTip: Every production change goes through an ECO with impact on cost, inventory, cert and quality, and a defined effectivity point.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What does 'effectivity' mean for an engineering change?","The point — a date or serial number — at which the change takes effect, which determines what happens to existing stock and in-progress builds."]}],
["Warranty", "How do you use field-return and warranty data as a PM?", "**Why they ask:** closing the loop from the field is a PM responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Returns and warranty claims are direct signal about quality and real-world use, so I track the return rate, the failure modes, and whether they cluster by date code, component lot or usage pattern. A rising return rate or a dominant failure mode triggers a root-cause effort with quality and engineering, and may drive a design or process change, a supplier conversation, or in a serious case a recall. I also feed what customers actually break or complain about into the next product's requirements. Warranty is a cost line I forecast and manage, and the field data is some of the most honest input I get.\"\n\nTip: Track return rate and failure modes by lot/date code; use them to drive fixes and to inform the next product's requirements.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What does a failure clustered by date code suggest?","A manufacturing or component-lot problem introduced at a point in time, which narrows the investigation and lets you contain just the affected units."]}],
["Quality metrics", "What quality metrics do you track for a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** PMs must speak the quality language.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At the factory I watch first-pass yield and the defect rate, often in defective parts per million, plus which test stations and failure modes dominate, because yield directly affects cost and ramp. In the field I track the return rate, failures per unit over time, and warranty cost. I also care about the Cpk on critical measurements, which shows how much margin the process has. I use these to decide where to invest — a low-yield station or a dominant failure mode gets a Pareto-driven fix — and to gate ramp decisions. Quality metrics tie directly to cost and customer satisfaction, so they're not just an ops concern.\"\n\nTip: First-pass yield and DPPM at the factory, return rate and warranty cost in the field, Cpk for process margin.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why does first-pass yield matter so much to a PM?","Low yield means scrap, rework and slower ramp, all of which raise cost and delay volume — so it directly hits margin and launch commitments."]}],
["ODM vs CM", "What's the difference between an ODM and a CM, and how do you choose?", "**Why they ask:** the manufacturing partner model shapes the program.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A contract manufacturer builds to your design — you own the design and IP and they provide manufacturing. An original design manufacturer designs and builds a product you brand, so you get to market faster with less engineering but less control and differentiation, often starting from their reference design. I choose a CM when the design is our differentiation and we want control and IP ownership, and an ODM when speed, lower engineering investment, or a commodity-ish product matter more than owning every detail. There's a spectrum in between. The choice affects cost, control, IP, time to market, and how much engineering we staff.\"\n\nTip: CM builds your design (you own it, more control); ODM designs it for you (faster, less control/differentiation) — choose by where your value is.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the IP risk with an ODM?","You're often building on their design and may not own key IP, and differentiation is limited since competitors can source similar ODM products."]}],
["Business case", "How do you build the business case for a new hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** PMs justify the investment.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I size the opportunity — the market, the target segment, and realistic volume — then model the economics: the development and tooling investment, the per-unit cost and target price, the resulting margin, and the volume needed to break even against that up-front spend. Hardware has real NRE and tooling and inventory cash, so the break-even volume and the payback period matter a lot, and I stress-test them against conservative demand. I weigh it against alternatives and strategic value, and I'm honest about the risks — demand, cost, schedule. The output is a clear recommendation with the assumptions visible, so leadership can decide with eyes open.\"\n\nTip: Model opportunity, NRE/tooling, unit cost and price, margin, and break-even volume/payback — stress-tested against conservative demand.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does break-even volume matter more for hardware than software?","Because large up-front tooling and NRE must be recovered per unit, so you need to sell a real quantity before the product is profitable — unlike near-zero-marginal-cost software."]}],
["Roadmap", "How do you build and manage a roadmap for hardware with long dev cycles?", "**Why they ask:** hardware roadmapping is different from software.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Hardware cycles are long — often a year or more from concept to volume — so the roadmap looks further out and can't rely on frequent releases to course-correct. I plan a cadence of product generations and sustaining updates, align it with component roadmaps and platform reuse so I'm not reinventing each time, and build in the long-lead decisions like silicon selection early. Because I can't patch hardware after it ships, I'm deliberate about what makes each generation and what waits, and I use firmware and accessories to add value between hardware generations. I keep the roadmap tied to strategy and revisit it as the market and technology move, but with the humility that commitments are expensive to change.\"\n\nTip: Plan generations and platform reuse, make long-lead silicon decisions early, and use firmware/accessories to add value between hardware refreshes.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you add value between hardware generations?","Firmware updates, new accessories, and software/services extend a shipped product's value without a new hardware cycle, bridging the long gap between generations."]}],
["Risk management", "How do you manage risk in a hardware program?", "**Why they ask:** hardware programs live or die on risk management.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep a live risk list across technical, supply, schedule and cost, rank each by likelihood and impact, and drive mitigations on the top ones rather than hoping. For the scary technical unknowns I push to de-risk early — build a breadboard, test the critical component, prove the hard part before the design depends on it. For supply I dual-source and watch lead times; for schedule I protect the critical path, usually tooling and long-lead parts, and hold contingency. The gates are where I force an honest risk review. The mindset is to confront the biggest risks early when they're cheap to address, not discover them at a late, expensive gate.\"\n\nTip: Rank risks by likelihood×impact, de-risk the hard technical unknowns early with prototypes, and protect the critical path (tooling/long-lead).", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What does 'de-risk early' look like concretely?","Build and test the riskiest part — a new sensor, a thermal design, a radio — on a breadboard before the whole design commits to it, so a failure is cheap to absorb."]}],
["Cross-functional", "How do you coordinate hardware, firmware and software teams on one product?", "**Why they ask:** integration across disciplines is a PM core skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A hardware product is really several programs — mechanical, electrical, firmware, software, and sometimes cloud — that have to converge on the same schedule, and they move at different speeds. I align them to shared milestones, especially the integration points like board bring-up where firmware meets hardware, and I make dependencies explicit so firmware isn't blocked waiting for a board or software waiting for an API. I plan for the fact that firmware and software can keep improving after the hardware freezes, so I phase features accordingly. Mostly it's keeping everyone pointed at the same dates, surfacing cross-team dependencies early, and resolving the trade-offs when one discipline's constraint hits another.\"\n\nTip: Align the disciplines to shared milestones and integration points, make cross-team dependencies explicit, and phase software to keep improving after HW freeze.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why phase features so software lands after hardware freeze?","Hardware must freeze for tooling and certification, but firmware and software can keep shipping, so you lock the hardware-dependent features and let the rest mature later."]}],
["Serviceability", "How do you decide how repairable or serviceable a product should be?", "**Why they ask:** serviceability affects cost, warranty and sustainability.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It's a trade-off. More serviceable — modular, accessible fasteners, replaceable battery and parts — lowers warranty and repair cost, extends product life, and increasingly matters for sustainability and right-to-repair expectations, but it can add cost, size and complexity versus a sealed, glued design that's cheaper and more rugged. I weigh the expected failure and wear modes — if the battery or a connector is the likely wear-out, making that serviceable pays off — against the manufacturing cost and the product's positioning. I also consider how it's serviced: user-replaceable, depot repair, or whole-unit swap, each with different design and cost implications.\"\n\nTip: Trade serviceability (lower warranty cost, longer life, right-to-repair) against unit cost, size and ruggedness — target the likely wear parts.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["When is a sealed, non-serviceable design the right call?","When ruggedness, water resistance, size or cost dominate and the failure rate is low enough that whole-unit swap is cheaper than designing for repair."]}]
]);

// Hardware Product Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-pm"] = (QB["hw-pm"] || []).concat([
["Specs vs requirements", "What's the difference between a requirement and a specification?", "**Why they ask:** PMs must keep the two straight.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A requirement states what the product must do or achieve from the user's and business's point of view — 'runs eight hours on a charge,' 'survives a one-meter drop,' 'costs under X.' A specification is the engineering answer to that — the battery capacity, the enclosure material and ribs, the component choices that deliver it. I own the requirements and make them testable and prioritized; engineering owns the specs that meet them. Keeping them separate matters: if I over-specify the how, I constrain engineering unnecessarily and may miss a better solution; if I under-specify the what, the product misses the need. So I say what and why, and let engineering own the how, verifying specs trace back to requirements.\"\n\nTip: Requirements = what/why (PM owns); specs = how (engineering owns) — don't over-specify the how.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of a PM writing the specs instead of requirements?","You constrain engineering to your solution and may block a better or cheaper one, and you blur accountability for whether the design actually meets the need."]}],
["Packaging and accessories", "How do you think about packaging and accessories for a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** they affect cost, experience and margin.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Packaging is part of the product experience and a real cost and logistics factor — it protects the unit through shipping, shapes the out-of-box experience, and its size drives freight and warehousing cost, so I design it to protect and delight without over-spending or over-sizing. Accessories — cases, cables, add-ons — can extend the product, improve the experience, and often carry higher margin than the core device, so they're both a revenue and an ecosystem play. I decide what's in-box versus sold separately based on the experience and the cost, and I plan accessory compatibility into the product so the ecosystem grows. Sustainability pressure also pushes packaging toward less plastic and smaller boxes.\"\n\nTip: Packaging protects and sells but drives freight cost; accessories extend the product and often carry higher margin.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why does package size matter beyond aesthetics?","It drives freight, warehousing and retail shelf cost per unit, so a smaller box can meaningfully lower logistics cost at volume."]}],
["Go to market", "How do you plan the go-to-market and channel for a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** launch is more than finishing the product.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I decide the channels — direct online, retail, distributors, or through partners — because each has different margin, inventory, and support implications, and hardware in retail needs channel stock, packaging that sells on a shelf, and returns handling. I align the launch with manufacturing readiness so there's enough stock to meet demand without a stockout or a glut, coordinate marketing and PR to the availability date, set pricing with channel margin in mind, and make sure support, documentation, spares and RMA processes are ready. The product being done isn't the launch — the whole commercial and operational machine has to be ready at the same moment.\"\n\nTip: Match channel choice to margin/inventory/support needs, and sync launch to real manufacturing readiness and support/spares.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can launching before supply is ready backfire?","Demand you generate hits stockouts, frustrating customers and the channel and wasting marketing spend — so you gate the announcement on real inventory."]}],
["Competitive teardown", "How do you use a competitive teardown?", "**Why they ask:** teardowns inform cost and design decisions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Tearing down a competitor's product tells me how they solved the problem — their component choices, their likely BOM cost, their design and manufacturing approach, and where they spent and saved. I use that to benchmark our cost and features, spot where they're vulnerable or ahead, and inform our own trade-offs. For example, if their teardown shows an expensive component we could match more cheaply, that's an opportunity; if they achieved something we can't, I need to understand how. It grounds competitive analysis in physical reality rather than spec sheets, and it's a standard input to positioning and cost targets. I pair it with market and pricing analysis.\"\n\nTip: A teardown reveals a competitor's BOM, design choices and cost structure — grounding competitive and cost decisions in reality.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What can you estimate from a teardown besides parts?","Approximate BOM cost, manufacturing approach, design priorities, and where they cut cost or invested — useful for benchmarking your own targets."]}],
["Recall decision", "A field failure could be a safety issue. How do you handle the recall decision?", "**Why they ask:** high-stakes judgment under pressure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Safety comes first and fast. I get the facts quickly — the failure mode, how many units are affected, the real risk of harm, and whether it's contained to a lot — working with quality, engineering and legal. If there's a genuine safety risk I don't slow-walk it; I move to contain, notify the right regulatory bodies as required, and plan the remedy — repair, replace or refund — honestly. I'd rather over-react to a safety issue than under-react. For a non-safety quality issue the calculus includes cost and reputation, but I never trade a real safety risk against cost. Clear, honest communication with customers and regulators protects people and the brand.\"\n\nTip: On a real safety risk, act fast to contain and remedy and notify regulators — never weigh a genuine safety hazard against cost.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["How do you scope which units are affected?","Trace the failure to its root cause — a component lot, date code or process window — and use serialization and build records to bound the recall to just those units."]}],
["Pricing", "How do you set the price for a new hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** pricing drives margin and positioning.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start from value — what the product is worth to the customer and how it's positioned against alternatives — not just cost, but I use cost as the floor since hardware has a real per-unit cost plus channel margin to cover. I look at competitive pricing, the segment, and whether the strategy is premium, value, or to seed an ecosystem where the device is priced thin and accessories or services carry margin. I check that the price supports a healthy gross margin at expected volume and leaves room for channel and promotions. I also consider price over the life — hardware prices often fall, so I plan the launch price and the glide path.\"\n\nTip: Price on value and positioning with cost-plus-channel as the floor; plan the price glide path over the product's life.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a razor-and-blades hardware pricing strategy?","Price the device thin to drive adoption and make margin on recurring accessories, consumables or services — common where an ecosystem or refills exist."]}],
["Stakeholders", "How do you keep executives and stakeholders aligned on a long hardware program?", "**Why they ask:** communication is half the PM job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Long programs drift and surprises are costly, so I over-communicate status honestly: where we are against the gates, the top risks and what I'm doing about them, and any change to cost, schedule or scope with its implication. I don't hide bad news — a slipping critical path or a cost overrun surfaced early can be managed; discovered late it can't. At decision points I bring a clear recommendation with the trade-offs and the data, so leadership can decide efficiently. Between those I keep a steady cadence so there are no shocks. The trust that comes from honest, consistent communication is what lets me get fast decisions when I need them.\"\n\nTip: Communicate status, top risks and changes honestly and on a cadence; surface bad news early with a recommendation.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why surface a schedule slip early even when it's uncomfortable?","Because early, options exist — reprioritize, add resource, adjust scope — while a slip revealed at a gate leaves no room to recover and erodes trust."]}],
["Beta program", "How do you run a beta or field trial for hardware?", "**Why they ask:** real-world validation before mass production.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I put near-final units — usually DVT or early PVT builds — in the hands of representative users in real environments, because lab testing never surfaces everything real use does: handling, edge conditions, usability, and failures that only appear over time or at scale. I define what I want to learn, instrument the units to collect data and issues, and set up a tight feedback loop. I weigh the findings by severity and how late it is — a usability tweak might wait for the next rev, a reliability or safety issue blocks production. The constraint is timing: beta has to happen early enough that I can still act, but on units representative enough to trust.\"\n\nTip: Put representative near-final units with real users early enough to act; triage findings by severity against how late it is to change.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why use DVT or PVT units for beta rather than rough prototypes?","So the feedback reflects the real product; rough prototypes have issues that won't ship and mask the ones that will."]}],
["Production ramp", "What happens during a production ramp and what's the PM's role?", "**Why they ask:** ramp is where plans meet reality.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Ramp is scaling from the first production builds to full volume, and it rarely goes perfectly — yield starts lower and climbs as the line stabilizes, early failures surface, and the supply chain has to keep pace. My role is to watch yield and the top failure modes daily, drive fast root-cause on anything blocking volume, and make the calls when a problem means holding shipments versus shipping with a contained fix. I manage the tension between hitting the launch date and not shipping quality problems, keep the demand and supply plans synced to the real ramp rate, and keep stakeholders informed. A good ramp is managed, not assumed.\"\n\nTip: Watch yield and failure modes daily, drive fast root-cause, and manage the hold-vs-ship calls while syncing demand to the real ramp rate.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does yield usually start low and climb during ramp?","The line, fixtures, operators and process are still being tuned; as issues are found and fixed, first-pass yield improves toward its steady state."]}],
["MOQ and inventory", "How do minimum order quantities and lead times affect your planning?", "**Why they ask:** component economics constrain the plan.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Many components have minimum order quantities and long lead times, so I often have to commit to buying a quantity of parts well before I know real demand, which ties up cash and creates obsolescence risk if the forecast is wrong or a design change strands the parts. I plan around it by ordering long-lead and high-MOQ parts early based on a committed forecast, favoring parts with reasonable MOQs where I can, and staging commitments so I'm not over-committed on an unproven product. It's a cash-flow and risk balance: enough parts to build and not stock out, without burying cash in inventory that might not sell or might be obsoleted by a change.\"\n\nTip: MOQs and long lead times force early cash commitments — balance stockout risk against tied-up cash and obsolescence from design changes.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How can a design change strand inventory?","If you've bought parts to an MOQ and then change the design, the old parts may become unusable, turning committed cash into write-off."]}],
["Prioritization", "How do you decide what to say no to on a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** prioritization and discipline define a good PM.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Hardware forces hard choices because every feature costs money, space, power, schedule and risk, and I can't patch it in later, so the bar for inclusion is high. I prioritize against the core user need and the business case: does this feature materially serve the target user or differentiate us, and is it worth its cost in BOM, complexity, and schedule risk? I protect the critical path and the cost target by cutting or deferring features that are nice but not central, and I'm explicit with stakeholders about why. Saying no to good ideas to keep the product focused, affordable and on time is a lot of the job.\"\n\nTip: Judge each feature against the core need and business case versus its cost in BOM/space/power/schedule — cut nice-but-not-central to protect cost and timeline.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is saying no harder but more important for hardware?","Because you can't add it later cheaply and every feature consumes cost, space, power and schedule — scope creep directly blows the cost target and the date."]}],
["Battery budget", "How do you manage a power or battery-life requirement across the design?", "**Why they ask:** battery life is a top cross-cutting hardware constraint.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Battery life is a system property, so I set it as a hard requirement and then manage a power budget that every subsystem draws from — the processor, radios, sensors, display — in active and sleep states, because sleep current often dominates total life. I make the budget visible so teams design to it, push firmware to aggressively use low-power modes, and weigh features against their power cost since a new sensor or a brighter display spends the budget. I validate it with real measurements under realistic use profiles, not just datasheet numbers, early enough to adjust battery size or cut power. It's a constant trade-off between capability, size, cost and life.\"\n\nTip: Treat battery life as a managed power budget every subsystem draws from; sleep current usually dominates, and validate under real use profiles.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does sleep current often dominate battery life?","Most devices spend the vast majority of time idle, so even a small sleep current, integrated over that time, can outweigh brief active bursts."]}],
["Regional variants", "How do you handle selling one product across multiple regions?", "**Why they ask:** global hardware has regional complexity.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Different regions mean different certifications, radio frequency allocations and power limits, mains voltages and plug types, languages, and sometimes regulatory content rules. I decide early whether it's one global design with configurable options — a universal power input, radios certified for each region, software-selected regional settings — or separate variants, because that choice drives cost, inventory complexity and certification effort. A single flexible design simplifies inventory and lets me ship anywhere, but may cost more per unit; regional variants are cheaper per unit but multiply SKUs, certifications and forecasting complexity. I plan the certification schedule and the regional launch sequence around lead times.\"\n\nTip: Decide early between one flexible global design and regional variants — it trades per-unit cost against SKU/cert/inventory complexity.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why can SKU proliferation from regional variants hurt?","More SKUs multiply forecasting, inventory, and certification effort and raise the risk of stranded stock in one region while another stocks out."]}],
["Sunsetting", "How do you decide to end-of-life a hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** managing the full lifecycle, not just launch.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I watch declining demand, eroding margin, rising support and warranty burden, and component obsolescence that would force a costly redesign to keep building. When the economics or the supply no longer justify it, I plan an orderly end-of-life: a last-time-buy or final build to meet remaining demand and service needs, a clear communication to customers and channel, a transition to a successor product, and a defined support and spare-parts commitment for the installed base so existing customers aren't stranded. The goal is to retire it deliberately — not run it into a component wall or a loss — while honoring the support promise to people who already bought it.\"\n\nTip: EOL on declining margin/demand or forced obsolescence; plan last-time-buy, successor transition, and a support/spares commitment to the installed base.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why commit to spares and support after EOL?","Customers keep using the product for years, so an honest support and service-parts plan protects the brand and meets obligations even after sales stop."]}],
["Thermal trade", "A product runs too hot to hit its performance target in the enclosure. How do you drive the trade-off?", "**Why they ask:** thermal is a classic hardware PM trade.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Thermal pits performance against size, cost, noise and reliability, so I frame the options clearly with engineering: throttle performance to stay cool, add cooling like a bigger heatsink, vents or a fan which adds cost, size or noise, enlarge or change the enclosure, or pick lower-power components. Each has a cost in the dimensions users care about, so I bring it back to the requirements — what performance do users actually need, how much size and noise will they tolerate, what's the cost ceiling — and pick the balance that best serves the target user and the business. I also make sure we validate the thermal solution across the real worst-case ambient, not just the bench.\"\n\nTip: Frame thermal options (throttle, add cooling, resize, lower-power parts) against the real user requirements and cost ceiling, and validate at worst-case ambient.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why validate thermals at worst-case ambient, not room temperature?","A design that's fine on the bench can throttle or fail in a hot room or enclosed space; real environments set the margin, not lab conditions."]}],
["Security", "What security and privacy considerations do you own for a connected hardware product?", "**Why they ask:** connected devices carry real security responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For a connected device I treat security as a product requirement, not an add-on: secure boot and signed firmware so only authorized code runs, encrypted communication, a safe update mechanism to patch vulnerabilities in the field, sensible defaults like no shared default passwords, and protecting any user data the device collects. I think about the whole lifecycle including how long we'll provide security updates, which is increasingly a regulatory expectation. Privacy means being clear about what data is collected and why, and minimizing it. Because I can't easily recall hardware, designing in the ability to update and respond to vulnerabilities is essential, and I weigh these against cost and complexity deliberately.\"\n\nTip: Make secure boot, encrypted comms, field-updatable firmware, no default passwords and data minimization product requirements — and commit to an update lifespan.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is a field-update mechanism central to device security?","You can't recall hardware for every vulnerability, so the ability to ship signed security patches over the air is what lets you respond after launch."]}]
]);

// Hardware Product Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-pm"] = (QB["hw-pm"] || []).concat([
["CM relationship", "How do you manage the relationship and negotiation with a contract manufacturer?", "**Why they ask:** the CM relationship shapes cost, quality and schedule.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat the CM as a partner whose incentives I need aligned with mine, not just a vendor to squeeze. I negotiate on total value — unit price, yes, but also NRE, tooling ownership, quality commitments, flexibility on volume, and lead times — and I keep pricing honest by understanding their cost structure and, where it matters, having a credible second source for leverage. Day to day I invest in clear communication, shared metrics on yield and quality, and solving problems together, because when a ramp issue hits I need them motivated to help, not hiding it. A CM that trusts the relationship and has fair margin delivers better quality and is there when something goes wrong.\"\n\nTip: Align incentives and negotiate total value (price, NRE, tooling, flexibility, quality), keep leverage with a credible alternative, and invest in the partnership for when problems hit.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just push the CM for the lowest possible price?","Squeezing their margin too hard invites corner-cutting on quality and poor responsiveness when you need help during a ramp or crisis — fair margin buys reliability."]}],
["Materials compliance", "How do you handle materials and environmental compliance like RoHS and REACH?", "**Why they ask:** substance rules are mandatory and supply-chain-wide.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Rules like RoHS restrict hazardous substances such as lead and certain flame retardants in electronics, and REACH governs chemicals of concern, and they apply across the whole bill of materials, so compliance is a supply-chain data problem. I require compliance declarations from every component supplier, make it a selection criterion, and track the data so I can prove the whole product conforms for each market we sell into. Non-compliance can block sale in a region or force a costly redesign, so I build it into part selection from the start rather than auditing at the end. There's also growing pressure around recyclability, packaging and conflict minerals that I factor into design and sourcing.\"\n\nTip: Collect compliance declarations across the whole BOM and make it a part-selection criterion from the start — it's a market-access gate, not an afterthought.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is materials compliance a supply-chain data problem?","Compliance depends on every component's materials, so you must collect and maintain declarations from all suppliers to certify the finished product."]}],
["Late change", "Late in the program a validation test reveals a flaw. How do you decide whether to fix it now or ship?", "**Why they ask:** the hardest real PM calls happen late.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I get the facts: what's the real-world impact of the flaw, how many users and how often, is it safety or just annoyance, and what are the fix options and their cost in schedule, tooling and risk. A safety or widespread functional flaw gets fixed even at the cost of a slip — shipping a known serious defect is far more expensive in warranty and reputation than a delay. A minor, rare issue might ship with a firmware mitigation now and a hardware fix in the next revision. I lay out the options and the trade-offs honestly for the stakeholders and drive a clear decision rather than letting schedule pressure make it by default. The worst outcome is shipping a serious flaw to hit a date.\"\n\nTip: Weigh real-world severity against fix cost; fix safety/widespread flaws even at a slip, mitigate minor ones — and never let schedule pressure decide by default.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["When is shipping with a firmware mitigation acceptable?","When the issue is minor and rare, a firmware workaround genuinely contains the user impact, and the proper hardware fix can follow in the next revision without risk to safety."]}]
]);
